# Discrete QUANTUM TUNNELING, a quantum walker crossing a classically-forbidden barrier, modeled and proved by the kernel.
# Classically a particle whose energy lies below a potential barrier CANNOT cross it: the barrier region is forbidden, so
# a walker arriving from one side stops at the wall and never appears on the far side. Quantum mechanically the story is
# different. The wavefunction does not stop at the wall. It decays inside the barrier but keeps a nonzero AMPLITUDE all the
# way through, so there is a real chance to find the particle on the far side. That leak of amplitude across a forbidden
# region is tunneling, the effect behind alpha decay, the scanning tunneling microscope, and the tunnel diode.
#
# This file models the DISCRETE essence of the effect, not the continuous Schrodinger equation. A one-dimensional chain of
# five sites holds a BARRIER in the middle: far-left, near-left, barrier, near-right, far-right. A CLASSICAL walker starting
# at far-left spreads to adjacent sites but is BLOCKED by the barrier: it can reach the left sites and stop, never entering
# or passing the wall. A QUANTUM walker spreads by the same nearest-neighbor hopping but its amplitude PASSES THROUGH the
# barrier, only attenuated, so its support reaches EVERY site including far-right. Reachability is the discrete shadow of
# "has nonzero amplitude here." The kernel proves the classical walker is blocked, the quantum walker tunnels to the far
# side, exhibits the far-right site as the gap between the two, and tracks an attenuation tally that drops across the
# barrier yet stays nonzero on the far side, strictly smaller than at the source. That gap is tunneling, made checkable.

load @term/seed/code/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both

# The five sites of the chain, left to right, with the barrier in the middle. A classical walker from far-left is stopped
# at the barrier. A quantum walker leaks its amplitude through it and reaches the far-right.
form site
  case far-left
  case near-left
  case barrier
  case near-right
  case far-right

# An attenuation tally, a Peano-style count of how much amplitude survives at a site. The source carries the most. Each
# hop costs one unit, and crossing the barrier costs more, but the count stays nonzero on the far side: that surviving
# nonzero amount is the tunneling amplitude.
form tally
  case none
  case some
    link prior, like tally

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map out of a site, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition serves
# the classical and quantum reachability tables and the right-neighbor step, so each reads as its own value table.
tree map-site
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# CLASSICAL REACHABILITY from far-left: the sites a below-barrier classical walker can occupy. It spreads left of the
# barrier (far-left and near-left) and is STOPPED at the wall. The barrier and everything beyond it are unreachable, so
# the classical particle is confined to its own side of the forbidden region.
task classical-reach
  take s, like site
  like flag
  fork case, read s
    fuse map-site, far-left, yes
    fuse map-site, near-left, yes
    fuse map-site, barrier, no
    fuse map-site, near-right, no
    fuse map-site, far-right, no

# QUANTUM REACHABILITY from far-left: the sites the quantum walker's amplitude reaches. Nearest-neighbor hopping carries
# the amplitude into the barrier, decays it there, and lets it continue out the far side, so EVERY site is reached,
# including the classically-forbidden far-right. This is the discrete signature of tunneling: nonzero amplitude all the
# way across.
task quantum-reach
  take s, like site
  like flag
  fork case, read s
    fuse map-site, far-left, yes
    fuse map-site, near-left, yes
    fuse map-site, barrier, yes
    fuse map-site, near-right, yes
    fuse map-site, far-right, yes

# The right neighbor of a site, the direction the walker hops to cross the chain. far-left to near-left to barrier to
# near-right to far-right, with the far-right pinned (the chain ends there). Nearest-neighbor hopping walks this map.
task hop-right
  take s, like site
  like site
  fork case, read s
    fuse map-site, far-left, near-left
    fuse map-site, near-left, barrier
    fuse map-site, barrier, near-right
    fuse map-site, near-right, far-right
    fuse map-site, far-right, far-right

# The surviving attenuation tally at each site for the quantum walker. Amplitude is highest at the source (far-left) and
# loses one unit per ordinary hop. Crossing the barrier costs an extra unit (the steep decay inside the forbidden region),
# so the far side carries less than a plain hop would leave, yet the count never reaches none: a nonzero amount tunnels
# through. The tower below is four-three-one-one against the source's five (one extra drop is spent at the barrier).
task amplitude
  take s, like site
  like tally
  fork case, read s
    case far-left
      send back
        make some
          bind prior
            make some
              bind prior
                make some
                  bind prior
                    make some
                      bind prior
                        make some
                          bind prior
                            make none
    case near-left
      send back
        make some
          bind prior
            make some
              bind prior
                make some
                  bind prior
                    make some
                      bind prior
                        make none
    case barrier
      send back
        make some
          bind prior
            make some
              bind prior
                make some
                  bind prior
                    make none
    case near-right
      send back
        make some
          bind prior
            make none
    case far-right
      send back
        make some
          bind prior
            make none

# Whether a tally is nonzero, that is, carries some surviving amplitude. The far side staying nonzero is the whole point:
# the amplitude is small there but not gone.
task is-nonzero
  take t, like tally
  like flag
  fork case, read t
    case none
      send back
        make no
    case some
      send back
        make yes

# Strict order on tallies: is the first STRICTLY below the second. Used to witness that the far-side amplitude is smaller
# than the source's while still nonzero, the attenuation across the barrier. Recurses on both arguments in step.
task tally-below
  take a, like tally
  take b, like tally
  like flag
  fork case, read b
    case none
      send back
        make no
    case some
      link more-b
      fork case, read a
        case none
          send back
            make yes
        case some
          link more-a
          send back
            call tally-below
              read more-a
              read more-b

# THE CLASSICAL WALKER IS BLOCKED BY THE BARRIER: classical-reach barrier = no and classical-reach far-right = no. A
# below-barrier classical particle cannot enter the forbidden region nor appear beyond it, so both the wall and the far
# side are unreachable. This is the classical impossibility tunneling defies.
rule classical-walker-cannot-enter-the-barrier
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call classical-reach
        make barrier
      make no
  calm hold

rule classical-walker-is-blocked-by-the-barrier
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call classical-reach
        make far-right
      make no
  calm hold

# THE QUANTUM WALKER TUNNELS THROUGH: quantum-reach far-right = yes. The amplitude leaks across the barrier and reaches the
# far side, the classically-forbidden site. This is THE tunneling result, the nonzero presence where a classical particle
# can never be.
rule quantum-walker-tunnels-through
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call quantum-reach
        make far-right
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE QUANTUM WALKER ALSO REACHES THE BARRIER ITSELF: quantum-reach barrier = yes. The amplitude does not vanish at the
# wall the way the classical particle does, it penetrates the forbidden region, which is what carries it out the far side.
rule quantum-walker-penetrates-the-barrier
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call quantum-reach
        make barrier
      make yes
  calm hold

# TUNNELING IS THE DIFFERENCE AT THE FAR SIDE: the quantum walker reaches far-right (yes) but the classical walker does not
# (no), exhibited together. The far-right is a site present in the quantum support and absent from the classical one, the
# concrete gap between the two reachabilities, and exactly the place the amplitude tunneled to.
rule tunneling-is-the-difference-at-the-far-side
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call quantum-reach
          make far-right
        call classical-reach
          make far-right
      make no
  calm hold

# THE QUANTUM WALKER REACHES EVERY SITE: quantum-reach s = yes, for every site s. The amplitude support is connected all
# the way across the chain, with no gap at the barrier. Proved by induction over the five sites, every case computing to
# yes. This is the monotone, unbroken support that distinguishes the quantum walker from the blocked classical one.
rule quantum-walker-reaches-every-site
  mark s, like site
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call quantum-reach
        read s
      make yes
  fold s

# REACHABILITY IS CARRIED ALONG THE HOP: if the quantum walker reaches a site, it reaches the site's right neighbor too.
# both (quantum-reach s) (quantum-reach (hop-right s)) = quantum-reach s, for every site. Since every site is reachable,
# the hop never leaves the support, so the amplitude propagates step by step through the barrier without breaking. Proved
# by induction over the sites.
rule reachability-propagates-through-the-hop
  mark s, like site
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call quantum-reach
          read s
        call quantum-reach
          call hop-right
            read s
      call quantum-reach
        read s
  fold s

# THE FAR-SIDE AMPLITUDE IS NONZERO: is-nonzero (amplitude far-right) = yes. The amount that tunneled through is small but
# real. A nonzero amplitude on the far side of a forbidden barrier is the entire content of the tunneling effect.
rule far-side-amplitude-is-nonzero
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-nonzero
        call amplitude
          make far-right
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE AMPLITUDE IS ATTENUATED ACROSS THE BARRIER: amplitude far-right is strictly below amplitude far-left. The far side
# carries less than the source, the decay the barrier imposes, yet the previous law shows it is still nonzero. Strictly
# smaller AND nonzero together is exactly attenuated tunneling: the amplitude survives the crossing but is diminished.
rule far-side-amplitude-is-attenuated
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call tally-below
        call amplitude
          make far-right
        call amplitude
          make far-left
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE BARRIER COSTS EXTRA: the amplitude just past the barrier (near-right) is strictly below the amplitude just before it
# (near-left). Crossing the forbidden region drops the surviving amount by more than an ordinary hop, the steep decay
# inside the barrier that makes tunneling rare. The drop is real but does not reach none, so the walker still gets across.
rule crossing-the-barrier-costs-extra
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call tally-below
        call amplitude
          make near-right
        call amplitude
          make near-left
      make yes
  calm hold
